.TH CCOL_FIXED_WIDTH_DATA_TYPE_SIZE 3 "2026-09-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size \- byte size of the C type that a ccol_data_type names
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/common.h>

size_t ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(ccol_data_type type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns sizeof() of the C type that the enumerator stands for. It does
this for every ccol_data_type that names one specific C type.
ccol_string, ccol_other_types, and any value that the function does not
recognize return 0. A key or a value of those types can correctly have
any size, so there is no one width to report.

A key_pair must carry this width for a key type of a fixed width. Every
chmap entry point and every cbmap entry point that takes such a pair
needs that width. Code that builds a cmap_pair by hand can call this
function to give the pair the correct size.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
type   The data type enumerator to size
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns sizeof() of the C type that matches the enumerator
Returns 0 if the type has no fixed width. The types with no fixed
width are ccol_string, ccol_other_types, and any value that the
function does not recognize
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
common.h defines this as a static inline function. The library exports
no symbol for it
The width is the sizeof() of this platform. It changes with the ABI for
long, for a pointer, and for long double
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
size_t width = ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(ccol_int);
int key = 42;
cmap_pair kp = { .ptr = &key, .size = width };
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_insert_elem (3)
